# gpu-vllm-t4-2b — authoritative configuration.
#
# The directory name is documentation; this file decides. Never copy a slot value
# out of the directory name into a CLI flag (root CLAUDE.md, @NAMING.md "The name
# is a claim"). `tpu.env` is the filename every rig in this tree uses, including
# the ones with no TPU in them — a wart kept for consistency, not a claim.
#
# Committed on purpose and deliberately NOT gitignored. A real environment
# variable always wins over it: dotenv does not overwrite and Makefiles use ?=.
#
# STATUS 2026-09-18: SERVING AND MEASURED. vLLM 0.29.0 on torch 2.13.0+cu130
# serves both E2B builds on this T4, and one sweep is recorded:
# `benchmarks/runs/2026-09-18-qat-vs-bf16-t4/` (bf16 vs QAT w4a16, input
# {512, 4096} x concurrency {1, 4, 8, 16}, 3 repeats, worst-cell cv 2.0%). Its
# REPORT.md is the source for every number this rig claims; numbers below that
# are not in it are MEASURED ON A SIBLING (`gpu-vllm-g4dn-2b`, same silicon) or
# ARITHMETIC, and say so.
#
# THE HEADLINE: the QAT w4a16 build is the default now. Marlin runs on SM 7.5
# in vLLM 0.29, and at 512-token prompts QAT beats bf16 in every cell measured
# — 72.3 vs 40.4 tok/s per stream at c=1, 215.9 vs 164.6 tok/s total at c=8 —
# because decode is bandwidth-bound and QAT reads 1.86 GB per token against
# 4.60 (ARITHMETIC, evidence/bytes-per-token.txt).
#
# WHY THIS RIG EXISTS. Two reasons, and neither is "another T4 rig".
#
#   1. IT IS A CONTROL-PLANE A/B AGAINST `gpu-vllm-g4dn-2b`. Same TU104 T4, same
#      15360 MiB, same 5001 MHz memory clock, same x86_64 host, same vLLM, same
#      checkpoint. What differs is that THE GPU IS ALREADY ATTACHED TO THE BOX
#      THIS RIG RUNS ON — there is no launch, no AMI, no spot bid, no SSM, and
#      no container. Slots 1 and 3 are the only slots that differ, which is what
#      @NAMING.md calls an A/B pair (cf. `local-llamacpp-1650ti-2b-q4_0` against
#      `gpu-llamacpp-g5g-2b-q4_0`).
#   2. IT IS THE THIRD DELIVERY of the same Turing patch, and the cheapest.
#      `gpu-vllm-g5g-2b` gets it in via a ~67-minute from-source build because
#      its arm64 image has no SM 7.5 kernels. `gpu-vllm-g4dn-2b` builds a derived
#      docker image with one COPY. Here there is no image at all: vLLM is a pip
#      install and the patch edits one file in site-packages. See
#      docs/turing-on-a-gce-t4.md.
#
# WHAT IS NOT NEW: nothing about the chip. Turing's 64 KiB shared-memory ceiling,
# the forced TRITON_ATTN backend and the float16 dtype are properties of the T4
# and of Gemma 4, already established on two siblings. Do not re-derive them
# here; read @HARDWARE.md "T4G" (the chip section) and @MODELS.md.

# --- Model -------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE QAT w4a16 BUILD IS SERVED BY DEFAULT, as of 2026-09-18. Must be a
# SAFETENSORS repo: vLLM cannot load a GGUF on any platform slot (verified
# 2026-09-02, @QUANTIZATION.md).
#
# THE RIG NAME CARRIES NO `-w4a16` VARIANT SLOT, and that is a known mismatch
# rather than an oversight: the directory was named for the reference build and
# still serves it on request (MODEL_BF16_NAME below). If QAT stays the default,
# @NAMING.md's variant rule says the name should follow — decide that there.
#
# An earlier version of this file called QAT "a lever with nothing to lift" here.
# That was right about CAPACITY and wrong about SPEED: it measured disk and VRAM,
# never tokens per second. Capacity still does not bind at this serving shape —
# bf16 leaves a 315,974-token KV pool — but decode is bandwidth-bound, and QAT
# reads 1.86 GB per token against 4.60. See the run's REPORT.md.
MODEL_NAME=google/gemma-4-E2B-it-qat-w4a16-ct

# MEASURED from the Hub 2026-09-04 by `local-vllm-cpu-2b`. IT SAVES 19%, NOT 75%
# — the checkpoint's own `ignore` list holds the vision tower and the embeddings
# at bf16, so only the linears are packed. NEVER size this family from
# weights/4; that under-predicts by ~3x. Model loading MEASURED 8.02 GiB.
MODEL_SAFETENSORS_BYTES=8316306646

# The reference bf16 build, kept for the A/B against `gpu-vllm-g4dn-2b`, which
# measured this checkpoint. Serve it by overriding MODEL_NAME and
# MODEL_SAFETENSORS_BYTES in the environment. 10246621918 B MEASURED from the Hub
# 2026-09-04; model loading MEASURED 9.8 GiB here.
MODEL_BF16_NAME=google/gemma-4-E2B-it
MODEL_BF16_BYTES=10246621918

# `check_host_capacity` compares MODEL_NAME against this "alternative". With QAT
# as the default the two are the same checkpoint, so its alternative-build line
# is redundant until the capacity code learns which build is which.
MODEL_W4A16_NAME=google/gemma-4-E2B-it-qat-w4a16-ct
MODEL_W4A16_BYTES=8316306646

# --- Hardware: the GPU -------------------------------------------------------
# Slot 3 is `t4`, THE GPU SKU, and that is the rule rather than an exception:
# @NAMING.md spells the EC2 *instance family* only on EC2 (`g5g`, `g4dn`, `g6`),
# and "everywhere else, `local` included — the GPU SKU". This is Compute Engine,
# so it is the SKU. Do not "correct" it to `g4dn` (that is the EC2 sibling) and
# do not invent an `n1` slot: @NAMING.md keeps cloud and machine-type names out
# of slot 3 entirely.
#
# MEASURED ON THIS HOST 2026-09-17 via nvidia-smi:
#   Tesla T4 · compute capability 7.5 · 15360 MiB · driver 615.71.09
#   TU104GL (lspci) · SM clock 1590 MHz · memory clock 5001 MHz · 70 W cap
#
# The memory clock matters because it is the one number that makes the A/B
# claim checkable: `gpu-vllm-g4dn-2b` and `gpu-vllm-g5g-2b` both MEASURED 5001
# MHz and 15360 MiB on their T4/T4G. This is the same part at the same clocks.
ACCELERATOR_TYPE=nvidia-tesla-t4
CHIP_COUNT=1
COMPUTE_CAPABILITY=7.5
GPU_TOTAL_MIB=15360

# One chip, so TP=1. Chip count is topology, not tensor-parallel size — they
# happen to agree here and are still separate settings (@NAMING.md).
TENSOR_PARALLEL_SIZE=1

# --- Hardware: the host ------------------------------------------------------
# NOT `local`. The machine is a Compute Engine VM, and @NAMING.md is explicit:
# "SSH into a cloud VM you provisioned and it keeps that VM's platform value."
# `local` is for a machine you own. So slot 1 is `gpu` — "a general-purpose GPU
# attached to a VM, any cloud".
#
# MEASURED ON THIS HOST 2026-09-17:
HOST_PLATFORM=gce
HOST_MACHINE_TYPE=n1-standard-2
HOST_ZONE=us-west2-b
HOST_CPU="Intel(R) Xeon(R) CPU @ 2.00GHz"
HOST_VCPU=2
HOST_RAM_TOTAL_BYTES=7797587968
HOST_SWAP_TOTAL_BYTES=0
HOST_GCC=14.2.0

# THREE FILESYSTEMS, AND THE TWO MULTI-GB WRITES LAND ON DIFFERENT ONES. This is
# the trap on this host and it caught the first draft of this rig: `df /` and even
# `df ~` report the 32 GB root disk with 4.23 GB free, and concluding "disk binds"
# from that is wrong, because `~/.cache` IS A SYMLINK to `/opt1/cache` on a
# separate 250 GB volume. MEASURED 2026-09-17:
#
#   /        33,570,021,376 B total,   4,232,474,624 B free  <- root, nearly full
#   /tmp      3,898,793,984 B total,   3,879,206,912 B free  <- pip's build dir
#   /opt1   263,086,084,096 B total, 249,202,933,760 B free  <- ~/.cache -> here
#
# So: the CHECKPOINT has 249 GB and is not a constraint. `pip` is, twice over —
# once for where the packages land and once for where it unpacks them. Never size
# this rig from a single `df`; `check_host_capacity` measures each target
# filesystem separately, by path.
HOST_DISK_ROOT_AVAIL_BYTES=4232474624
HOST_DISK_TMP_AVAIL_BYTES=3879206912
HOST_DISK_OPT1_AVAIL_BYTES=249202933760

# THE HOST SHIPS WITH NO SWAP, AND vLLM CANNOT LOAD E2B WITHOUT SOME. MEASURED
# 2026-09-18: the default loader was OOM-killed on host RAM twice — EngineCore at
# 4.0 GB anon RSS, host peak 7,252 MiB of 7,800 — during "Loading model from
# scratch", before any KV allocation. A 16 GB swapfile on /opt1 fixed it; peak
# swap then MEASURED 6.2-6.8 GB for bf16 and 4.8 GB for QAT. The swapfile is NOT
# in fstab, so after a reboot it is gone and the load fails again:
#
#   sudo fallocate -l 16G /opt1/swapfile && sudo chmod 600 /opt1/swapfile
#   sudo mkswap /opt1/swapfile && sudo swapon /opt1/swapfile
#
# HOST_SWAP_TOTAL_BYTES above records the host as provisioned, not as patched.
# `--load-format fastsafetensors` is NOT a way around it here: it looks for an
# unversioned `libcudart.so` and the pip CUDA wheels ship only `.so.13`.
#
# 2 vCPU IS BELOW EVERY MEASURED T4 SIBLING (g4dn.xlarge had 4, g5g.4xlarge 16)
# AND IS NOT EXPECTED TO CAP THROUGHPUT. The g4dn run's own notes reason that
# decode is GPU-bandwidth-bound at these rates and that a 42-48% gap between two
# spec-identical GPUs "CANNOT be attributed to the host CPU". Where it is
# expected to cost is ONE-TIME ENGINE STARTUP: that run spent 82.39 s of a
# 150.90 s engine init in compilation on 4 vCPU. ARITHMETIC, NOT MEASURED —
# budget roughly double here and treat it as startup, not serving.

# --- Turing (SM 7.5): the dtype policy is not a preference -------------------
# Turing has NO bfloat16 datapath and NO fp8. float16 is correct because it is
# what EXECUTES.
#
# STATE THE REASON PRECISELY, because the wrong reason invites someone to delete
# the guard: bfloat16 does NOT fail. MEASURED on `gpu-vllm-g5g-2b` 2026-08-12 —
# PyTorch upconverts, vLLM logs `Casting torch.bfloat16 to torch.float16` and
# proceeds. A silent cost is worse than an error. (@HARDWARE.md still says
# `--dtype bfloat16` is "a hard failure" on this part; that line is stale and
# should be corrected there, not restated here.)
DTYPE=float16

# `auto` keeps KV at the model dtype, i.e. float16. fp8 KV is NOT REACHABLE on
# Turing — no datapath at all. int8 is this part's only low-precision compute
# win (@HARDWARE.md) and vLLM does not expose it as a KV cache dtype.
KV_CACHE_DTYPE=auto

# There is deliberately NO VLLM_ATTENTION_BACKEND here. MEASURED on the G5g
# sibling 2026-08-12: vLLM does not recognize the variable ("Unknown vLLM
# environment variable detected") AND it forces TRITON_ATTN for Gemma 4 anyway,
# because the model's head dims are heterogeneous (sliding 256 / full 512) and
# only FA4 or Triton handle that. Setting it would be dead config.
#
# THE BACKEND IS NOT THE KNOB. The tile size inside the forced Triton kernel is,
# and that is what patch_triton_turing.py changes.

# Headroom under Turing's hard 65,536 B per block. The tile arithmetic in the
# patch does not account for the kernel's accumulators, so budgeting the full
# limit still overflows. This is the value the G5g sibling served with.
TURING_SMEM_BUDGET=60000

# --- Serving shape -----------------------------------------------------------
# These four are the sibling's MEASURED-good configuration, carried verbatim so
# that a first run here is comparable to `2026-08-30-first-serve-g4dn` rather
# than merely plausible. Changing one before the first run forfeits the A/B.
#
# At this shape the sibling MEASURED, on the same silicon:
#   weights 9.8 GiB of 15.0 GiB usable · GPU KV cache 329,579 tokens
#   max concurrency 20.12x at 16,384 tokens/request
#   time-to-healthy 346 s · engine init 150.90 s (82.39 s compilation)
#   peak 243.93 tok/s output at c=16 · 42.36 tok/s single-stream
#
# DO NOT SIZE KV FROM 18 KiB/TOKEN HERE. @MODELS.md derives 18,432 B/token from
# the geometry and it is right about the geometry, but the vLLM CUDA path reports
# about half: 329,579 tokens against a ~2.95 GiB pool is ~9,622 B/token, the
# same figure the L4 run produced. @MODELS.md carries this as an OPEN
# DISCREPANCY — size from the engine's own allocation log, not from either.
GPU_MEMORY_UTILIZATION=0.90
MAX_MODEL_LEN=16384
MAX_NUM_SEQS=8

# --- Endpoint ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Known before the process starts. This is one of the two places the root
# CLAUDE.md's "never hardcode an endpoint" rule does not apply: there is no
# QR -> node -> external IP chain to walk, because the server runs here.
#
# 127.0.0.1, not 0.0.0.0. The sibling binds 0.0.0.0 because a remote instance is
# useless otherwise; this process is on the same host as its client, and this box
# has a public IPv4.
HOST=127.0.0.1
PORT=8000
ENDPOINT=http://127.0.0.1:8000

# --- Install: which interpreter, and onto which disk ------------------------
# THIS IS THE PART THAT IS SPECIFIC TO THIS HOST, and it is not the part a fork
# of an EC2 rig would think to look at. There are TWO pythons here and the wrong
# one is the default:
#
#   python3            -> pyenv 3.12.13, site-packages on `/`      4.23 GB free
#   /usr/bin/python3.13 -> 3.13.5, user site on `/opt1/pyuser`    249.20 GB free
#
# The second is the one with the room, and somebody has already been using it
# that way: `/opt1/pyuser`, `/opt1/pipcache` and `/opt1/tmp` all predate this rig
# (mtimes 2026-07-30). So this rig targets THAT interpreter explicitly rather
# than inheriting whatever `python3` happens to mean.
#
# THE PROJECT RULE STILL HOLDS AND IS NOT BEING BENT: no virtualenv. A relocated
# PYTHONUSERBASE is not a venv — it is the same interpreter's user site on a
# different disk, and site-packages stays system-wide, which is the property the
# root CLAUDE.md actually cares about (the rigs ship as a docker image).
PYTHON_BIN=/usr/bin/python3.13
PYTHONUSERBASE=/opt1/pyuser

# pip MUST be redirected, and BOTH of these are load-bearing on this host. /tmp
# has 3.88 GB and a CUDA torch wheel plus its nvidia-cu13 dependencies do not
# unpack into that, so a default `pip install` fails with ENOSPC in a build
# directory rather than anywhere obvious.
PIP_CACHE_DIR=/opt1/pipcache
TMPDIR=/opt1/tmp

# WHAT WAS INSTALLED THERE ON 2026-09-17 WAS BROKEN: a CUDA vLLM 0.26.0 on
# `torch 2.11.0+cpu`, so `import vllm` raised `ImportError: libcudart.so.13`.
# Neither package being present was evidence of anything — `verify_gpu_arch`
# asks the interpreter, never the directory listing.
#
# FIXED 2026-09-18, then upgraded. What is installed now is in
# `benchmarks/runs/2026-09-18-qat-vs-bf16-t4/evidence/packages.txt`:
# vllm 0.29.0, torch 2.13.0+cu130 (vLLM pins it exactly; torch 2.14 exists and
# would break vLLM's compiled ops), transformers 5.17.0, triton 3.7.1. The
# upgrade, which is what to re-run for the next vLLM:
#
#   PYTHONUSERBASE=/opt1/pyuser PIP_CACHE_DIR=/opt1/pipcache TMPDIR=/opt1/tmp \
#     /usr/bin/python3.13 -m pip install --user --break-system-packages -U \
#     --upgrade-strategy eager \
#     --extra-index-url https://download.pytorch.org/whl/cu130 vllm==<version>
#
# Do NOT `--no-deps` a torch swap: that skips cuDNN and the other nvidia-cu13
# runtime packages, and `import torch` then fails on `libcudnn.so.9`. And re-run
# `apply_turing_patch` after ANY vLLM change — the reinstall reverts the clamp.
VLLM_PIP_SPEC=vllm

# SETTLED 2026-09-18: THE PyPI cu130 WHEELS DO CARRY SM 7.5. torch reports
# ['sm_75', 'sm_80', 'sm_86', 'sm_90', 'sm_100', 'sm_120'], a float16 matmul runs
# on the device, and vLLM 0.29's Marlin (min capability 75) runs the QAT build.
# No from-source build is needed on this host, and one would not help: the hot
# path is Triton attention (JIT-compiled here), cuBLAS and Marlin.

# DOCKER IS NOT THE ROUTE HERE, and that is MEASURED rather than chosen. docker
# 29.8.1 is installed but registers only `runc` and `io.containerd.runc.v2` —
# there is no `nvidia` runtime and neither nvidia-container-runtime nor
# nvidia-ctk is present, so `docker run --gpus all` cannot see the GPU. The
# sibling's derived-image route is unavailable, not merely expensive.

# CUDA 13 IS NOT AN ADDITIONAL OBSTACLE. It dropped Maxwell, Pascal and Volta;
# Turing survived (@HARDWARE.md). The driver here reports CUDA UMD 13.4, and the
# nvidia-cu13 packages already on the box are consistent with that.
CUDA_DRIVER_VERSION=615.71.09

# TORCH_CUDA_ARCH_LIST, VLLM_IMAGE, VLLM_PATCHED_IMAGE, ROOT_VOLUME_*, DLAMI_*,
# INSTANCE_TYPE, AWS_REGION and HF_SECRET_ID are GONE, not blank. Nothing here
# builds, pulls an image, launches an instance, resolves an AMI or reads a secret
# manager. An inert setting that looks meaningful is worse than an absent one —
# that is the lesson `local-vllm-cpu-2b` was created to record.
